  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECTS_PLAN` | Operator declines the migration plan — stop; nothing is written |
    | `H_NOT_V1` | `sdd-state` does not report `FLOW_VERSION=v1` — there is nothing to migrate; return control |
    | `H_VERIFICATION_FAIL` | Final verification cannot reach v2 + clean and the gap is not recoverable in-flow — stop, report state, leave git baseline for rollback |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_SCAN">
      <Goal>Inventory the v1 repo deterministically — dry-runs only, no writes.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Confirm `FLOW_VERSION=v1` via `sdd-state` (else `H_NOT_V1`). Gather with tools, not by eye:
        `sdd-migrate plan --all .` (DRY-RUN — how many units, one per spec, and their ticket counts;
        orphan tickets surface here) and `sdd-migrate anchors --all .` (DRY-RUN — which tickets would
        be anchored / already are). Report the shape to the operator: units, tickets, orphans.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_LAYER">
      <Goal>Materialize the migration plan as an on-disk layer — the source of truth every later step consumes.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run `sdd-migrate plan --all . --write`. This scaffolds `migration/` mirroring `specs/`: one
        `<spec-base>.migration.md` per spec — named from the spec's own filename with `.migration`
        inserted before the extension (`demo.spec.md` → `demo.spec.migration.md`) — carrying: a
        generated Inventory (facts a rescan can re-derive); a Section Map pre-filled by curated
        heading rules, `UNMAPPED` rows left for the agent; a Ticket Map pre-filled tolerantly from
        each ticket's v1 Meta (a New-ID guess when the v1 Meta already carries ACR/slug-shaped
        fields — a best-effort seed, not an authoritative fill, the agent still reviews and corrects
        it in STEP_3) with the Diagram Plan left fully to the agent; a per-unit step checklist — plus
        `migration/README.md` (the global G1–G8 order and the unit table). Explain to the operator,
        in plain Russian: the plan is files, not chat — it survives session breaks, it is verified
        mechanically (`plan --verify`), and its units are self-sufficient job cards that can be
        handed to parallel agents. Nothing outside `migration/` is written here.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_ANCHORS">
      <Goal>Make v1 tickets visible to the v2 tools.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run `sdd-migrate anchors --all . --write` (deterministic — wraps canonical sections in
        `<!--SECTION:-->` markers; idempotent, skips already-anchored). Verify with `sdd-check` (anchor
        balance clean).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_FILL_MAPS">
      <Goal>Close the gaps the tool could not resolve — the Section Map arrives pre-filled; the agent reviews it and fills what a curated rule did not recognize.</Goal>
      <Action>
        `sdd-migrate plan --write` already ran `mapHeadingToSection` against every source heading and
        filled the **Section Map** for every heading a curated rule recognizes (action + target
        SECTION, e.g. `Module Vision` → `MODULE_VISION`); a heading no rule recognizes is left with
        target `UNMAPPED` — that row, and only that row, is this step's work. For each unit
        (parallelizable — a unit file is the worker's whole context): resolve every `UNMAPPED` row
        (keep / rename / merge / split / drop + the real target SECTION, from the scope-type's
        `*-spec-structure.xml`), spot-check the pre-filled rows against the source (a curated rule can
        be wrong on an atypical heading — fix it, do not defer to STEP_7), review and correct the
        **Ticket Map**'s tolerant v1-Meta pre-fill for every row (a row with no usable v1 Meta stays
        empty for the agent to fill from scratch): New ID `<ACR>-<slug>` — `ACR` UPPERCASE, `slug`
        kebab-case from `Meta.Purpose`, unique repo-wide (`NEW_ID_REGEX` gates the grammar — the same
        slug twice means «one feature»; destination computed from the ID) — and the **Diagram Plan**
        (which diagram carries the mandatory Overview and from what content it is built; extra
        diagrams by judgment). Set the unit `**Status:** MAPPED`.
        Gate: `sdd-migrate plan --verify` — inventory unchanged, maps complete, zero `UNMAPPED`
        targets remaining, action vocabulary respected, slug grammar valid, zero cross-unit slug
        collisions. Fix and re-verify until clean.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_ACK">
      <Goal>Operator sees the whole migration before anything outside `migration/` changes.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Present the verified layer, in plain Russian, facts separate from proposals
        (`AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE`): units and their section/ticket maps, the derived ID map, which
        decisions will merge (STEP_7), what gets simplified beyond a 1:1 copy, and the files+code the
        ID replacement will touch (from `sdd-migrate ids --from-plan` DRY-RUN counts). The operator
        confirms or edits (a scope to skip, a naming preference, a decision to keep) —
        `AX_OPERATOR_AGREEMENT`; `H_OPERATOR_REJECTS_PLAN` on decline. On go-ahead set units
        `**Status:** APPROVED`. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_5_IDS">
      <Goal>Replace Task-IDs across specs, tickets, and code — mechanically.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run `sdd-migrate ids --from-plan --write` (the map is derived from the approved Ticket Maps).
        The tool replaces exact IDs on word boundaries only — never a blind `TSK-[0-9]+` pattern,
        never non-IDs (`UTF-8`), never `D-NNN` / `FR-NN` / scope-prefixed requirement IDs — and gates
        itself on zero old IDs remaining. Then `sdd-check --all .` clean.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_6_MOVE">
      <Goal>Relocate tickets co-located and build the indexes — one scope at a time.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per scope: `sdd-migrate move --scope <scope> --write` — relocates every ticket to its
        Ticket-Map destination (`git mv`, history kept): module co-location when the unit has a
        module (`specs/<scope>/<module>/...`); a flat, scope-level destination is legal for a scope
        with no modules — the Ticket Map's destination decides, not a hardcoded module assumption.
        It rewrites every relative markdown link that
        pointed at a moved ticket (inside the moved tickets themselves and inside every other file in
        the same zones that referenced them by path). Backstop: `sdd-check` resolves ticket→spec
        references (`SDD_BROKEN_SPEC_REF` / `SDD_BROKEN_SPEC_ANCHOR`) and spec→spec links under
        `--all`, so a broken reference into a spec surfaces at the gate — but ticket→ticket links and
        arbitrary markdown links have no mechanical check yet: a link syntax the rewriter does not
        recognize, or a referencing file outside the zones, stays a manual follow-up. It also
        scaffolds `<module>.3-tasks.md` +
        `<scope>.3-tasks.md` from ticket Meta (`MODULE_TASKS_INDEX_STRUCTURE` /
        `SCOPE_TASKS_INDEX_STRUCTURE`; the Cascade Table slot stays for the agent), and automatically
        removes the now-emptied `tasks/<scope>/` — no separate cleanup step. Together (index present
        + tasks dir gone) that mechanically flips
        the scope to v2 — `sdd-check` detects flow per scope, so the migrated scope is now checked
        strictly while unmigrated neighbours stay lenient. A blocked move (unfilled map, stray
        ticket) stops that scope, not the run.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_7_RESTRUCTURE">
      <Goal>Transform each spec to the v2 document structure — the per-unit semantic work.</Goal>
      <Action>
        For each unit (parallelizable — units do not share files; repo-wide mechanical steps are
        already done): execute the unit's S4–S6 checklist. Restructure the spec per its Section Map:
        target section order from the scope-type's `*-spec-structure.xml` (general → specific,
        arc42/C4), headings without numbers, heavy sections folded under `<details>`, the Overview
        section carrying its diagram per the Diagram Plan. Restructuring reorders and clarifies what
        the source ALREADY says — per the Mission's out-of-scope line, migration only transforms what
        already exists: do not author a new section (Golden DX, FR, or any other) the source spec
        never had; a section the target structure calls for but the source lacks stays absent, marked
        for `evolve`/`sdd-continue` to fill later, never silently invented here. Collapse converged
        decisions via
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/compression.directive.xml")` (superseded marked
        per `AX_PIVOT_REQUIRES_SUPERSESSION`, never silently dropped; merges propagated to code
        references) — a Decision Log entry's TEXT is changed only through supersession, never edited
        in place. Rewrite operator prose to flat engineering Russian in the same pass, per
        `AX_MIGRATION_COMPREHENSION_PASS` — decode every empty label and code-only term from the
        actual code, replace every metaphor with the literal mechanism, never guess (code / IDs /
        status tokens / BDD keywords / paths stay English).
        Gate per scope: `sdd-check --all specs/<scope>` — strict v2 structure, folds, real mermaid
        parse, and the language lint (`SDD_LANGUAGE_CALQUE`) that gates the comprehension pass above
        mechanically — clean. Set the unit `**Status:** DONE`.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_8_VERIFY">
      <Goal>Prove the migration is complete; retire the working layer.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Final gate, all green or `H_VERIFICATION_FAIL`:
        `sdd-state` → `FLOW_VERSION=v2` · `sdd-check --all .` clean · zero original Task-IDs and zero
        `*.task-*.md` names remain (`sdd-migrate ids --from-plan` DRY-RUN reports nothing to do) ·
        every unit `**Status:** DONE` · per migrated module,
        `gennady lint --spec=<module-spec> --inventory-reverse <module-code-dir>` clean — the spec's
        Entity Inventory must match the code's real exports, not the migration's rewritten
        description of them. With the operator's confirmation remove the `migration/` layer (its
        content is now embodied in the repo; git history keeps the record). Return control to the
        caller — script readiness is the router's separate gate.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

  <ReferenceData>
    **What changes:** `tasks/<scope>/<module>/<m>.task-NN.md` → co-located
    `specs/<scope>/<module>/<m>.task.<ACR>-<slug>.md` + `*.3-tasks.md` indexes · `TSK-NN` (or
    `<ACR>-NNN`) → `<ACR>-<slug>` · tickets gain `<!--SECTION:-->` anchors · spec sections
    restructured to the v2 order (Overview + diagram, no heading numbers, heavy detail folded) ·
    operator-prose → flat Russian · redundant decisions collapsed.

    **What is NOT touched:** `D-NNN` (decision IDs, spec-local) stay · `FR-NN` / `NFC-NN` and
    scope-prefixed requirement/scenario IDs stay (optional later reformat to BDD `<ACR>-REQ-N`) ·
    `UTF-8` / `UTF-16` and similar are NOT IDs — never replaced.

    **Tooling state — all four mechanical modes are real tools:** `sdd-migrate plan` (generate /
    `--verify` the migration layer — the Section Map arrives pre-filled by `mapHeadingToSection`; the
    Ticket Map's New-ID arrives pre-filled tolerantly from each ticket's v1 Meta where it already
    carries ACR/slug-shaped fields — a best-effort seed the agent still reviews, never authoritative;
    `--verify` fails on any leftover `UNMAPPED` target or a New ID that fails `NEW_ID_REGEX`, which
    requires `<ACR>` UPPERCASE) · `sdd-migrate anchors` (section markers) · `sdd-migrate ids`
    (word-boundary ID replacement, `--from-plan`, self-gating on zero leftovers) · `sdd-migrate move`
    (per-scope relocation to the Ticket Map's destination — module co-location when the unit has a
    module, a flat scope-level destination legal for a scope without modules — + index scaffolding +
    relative markdown-link rewriting inside every moved ticket and every referencing file in the same
    zones + automatic removal of the now-emptied `tasks/<scope>/`). What stays semantic — closing
    `UNMAPPED` rows and correcting the Ticket Map pre-fill (STEP_3) and the
    restructure/compression/comprehension pass (STEP_7) — is agent work executed against a verified
    plan and gated by strict per-scope `sdd-check` plus, at the final gate, `gennady lint
    --inventory-reverse` per migrated module (STEP_8).

    **Mixed state is first-class:** `sdd-check` detects the flow version per scope. On a v1 repo a
    scope counts as migrated (v2, strict) only positively: `tasks/<scope>/` removed AND the
    co-located `<scope>.3-tasks.md` present — both produced by `sdd-migrate move`. A scope that
    merely never had tasks stays lenient until the repo flips. Migration therefore runs one scope at
    a time — each migrated scope is checked strictly immediately, no waiting for the whole repo.

    **Parallel dispatch:** unit files are self-sufficient — STEP_3 and STEP_7 fan out one unit per
    agent (units never share files); the repo-wide mechanical steps (anchors / ids / move) run
    centrally between the fan-outs, per `migration/README.md`'s G1–G8 order.
  </ReferenceData>

